# 20. Deployment Requirements

## Android

- Build a debug APK for development.
- Test the debug build on a physical device.
- Create a signed release build only after the release checklist passes.
- Configure package visibility and application intents according to Android requirements.
- Remove development-only configuration from production builds.

## Backend

- Deploy FastAPI to a suitable hosting environment.
- Configure the AI provider's credentials securely.
- Enable HTTPS.
- Configure logs, monitoring, and request limits.
- Set up database access only if persistent data is required.
- Define backup and deletion policies where data is stored.

## Environment Configuration

Development and production environments must use separate configuration.

A development endpoint such as `http://10.0.2.2:8000/` may be appropriate for a local Android emulator, but it is not a production backend address.

Production must use the real deployed HTTPS endpoint.

# 21. Third-Party Integration Rules

## Instagram

Use only supported Meta developer APIs and approved permissions. Confirm the exact functionality available to the app before committing to an in-app Instagram feed.

## WhatsApp

Opening the installed WhatsApp application is distinct from sending messages or accessing conversations. Message-related functionality must use a supported and authorized mechanism, such as an applicable official API or user-confirmed share flow.

## Facebook

Use official supported APIs for Facebook-specific data and actions. Launching the Facebook app does not grant permission to read its private content.

## YouTube

Use supported Android intents, links, or authorized APIs according to the intended feature.

## AI Provider

Use a configured AI provider through the backend. Handle authentication failures, rate limits, unavailable services, and model errors without pretending that the request succeeded.

